faq:
  - q: "Is 40 too old to advance in your career?"
    a: "No. Many of the highest-earning and most senior career moves happen in a professional's 40s — VP, SVP, C-suite, Board, and high-value consulting often require the experience only a 40-something has. The challenge in your 40s is not relevance — it is positioning. Professionals in their 40s who struggle often have strong experience and weak presentation: an outdated LinkedIn, a resume that reads like a job description, and interview narratives that do not translate depth into clear business value."
  - q: "How do I deal with ageism in the job search at 40?"
    a: "Positioning over hope. The most effective defense against ageism is a resume that leads with outcomes, not dates; a LinkedIn that signals currency (recent projects, modern skills, active posting); and interview narratives that demonstrate strategic thinking, not just historical experience. Practically: do not include jobs from more than 15 years ago; use a contemporary resume format; lead interviews with what you are building now, not what you built in 2008. Target companies with 40+ leadership — they signal cultural openness to your cohort."
  - q: "What career moves make the most sense at 40?"
    a: "Four high-value paths: (1) Level-up within your function — if you are a Director and not yet VP, 40s is the window. (2) Move to a smaller company at a higher title — a VP at a 200-person company is often more powerful and better compensated than a Director at a Fortune 500. (3) Executive MBA or credential if the goal is a function change (finance to strategy, operations to general management). (4) Consulting or fractional executive — 40s is when your network and expertise make independent work viable. Each path has different ROI depending on your goals."
  - q: "Should I pursue an MBA in my 40s?"
    a: "Only if the specific outcomes you want require it. An executive MBA in your early 40s can open C-suite doors in strategy and general management — particularly if you come from a technical function (engineering, finance) and want to transition to broader leadership. The ROI calculation: MBA cost ($100K–$200K) vs. the salary delta it produces in your specific career path. For most professionals, targeted coaching, positioning, and network investment produce better ROI at 40 than a degree."
  - q: "How do I maximize my earning potential in my 40s?"
    a: "Three moves: (1) Move to market rate if you are under. Your 40s should be your highest-compensation decade — if you are below market, close it aggressively now. Every year below market in your 40s is expensive at a high base. (2) Equity and total comp negotiation. At the Director, VP, and SVP level, equity and bonus are where the real money is. Most professionals undervalue and underprice equity in negotiations. (3) Parallel income: advisory roles, consulting, speaking, board observer seats — these add $30–150K per year for senior professionals who build them systematically."
